name: pattern_condense
description: >
  The shortest way to write a rhythm down. Three boxes: four rhythms drawn against their own
  condensations, a live check that the search invents no structure that is not there, and the
  loop closed from audio — samples in, a named figure out.

  `patCondense` searches Field's own mini-notation for the shortest string that plays the
  rhythm it is given, and it accepts a candidate only by playing it: every one goes back
  through `pat(...)` and its events are compared to the target's. That is why box 1 draws
  pairs rather than printing strings — the lower lane of a pair IS the string in its label,
  rendered, so a search that is wrong shows up as two lanes that do not coincide.

  Every box puts each output on its own channel. That is not decoration: boxes 1 and 3
  originally wrote the roll and then the table both to `main`, and the second silently
  overwrote the first, so the figure — the whole point — was never visible. Two writes to one
  channel is not an error, which is why nothing caught it and opening the document did.

  This transcript is what built `dev_docs/agents/demos/pattern_condense/metadata.edn`;
  re-running it rebuilds that document. See `dev_docs/PAT_CONDENSE.md` and the section on
  `public/docs/pattern_language.md`.
operations:
  - create:
      box: condense_basics
      name: 1 - Condense a rhythm
      frame: { x: 60.0, y: 60.0, w: 400.0, h: 136.0 }
      properties:
        code: |


          // 1 - Condense a rhythm.
          //
          // patCondense searches Field's own mini-notation for the shortest string that plays the
          // rhythm you hand it, and it accepts a candidate only by playing it: every one goes back
          // through pat(...) and its events are compared to the target's. So the lower lane of each
          // pair below IS the string in its label, rendered. If a pair does not coincide, the search
          // is wrong, and that is a thing to see rather than a thing to be told.
          //
          // POKE THIS: change a line in `rhythms`, or add one. A mask you invent comes back either as
          // a named figure - x(p,s) for Euclidean, x(kN) / x(gN) for Kolakoski - or spelled out, and
          // which one you get is decided by character count and nothing else.
          val rhythms = listOf(
              "x ~ ~ x ~ x ~ ~",
              "x ~ ~ x x ~ x ~ ~ x ~ ~ x x ~ x",
              "bd [hh hh] sd [cp cp]",
              "a b c d e c a d"
          )

          val found = showPatCondense(*rhythms.toTypedArray(), channel = "roll")

          val rows = rhythms.zip(found).joinToString("\n") { (source, c) ->
              "| `$source` | `${c.notation}` | ${c.notation.length}/${c.flat.length} | " +
                  "${"%.2f".format(c.compression)} | ${c.structure.joinToString(", ")} |"
          }

          markdown(
              "main",
              """
              ### ${found.count { it.exact }} of ${found.size} condensed exactly

              | given | shortest string that plays it | chars | ratio | structure |
              |---|---|---|---|---|
              $rows

              The last row is why the other three mean anything. `a b c d e c a d` has no repeat at any
              split the grammar can take, so nothing shortens it and it comes back as itself. A search
              scored on description length has to be shown *not* naming a figure as well as naming one.

              Bounds this run used: ${found.first().limits}

              The figure is on the **roll** channel beside this one. These rhythms are abstract — `x`,
              `bd`, `a` — so there is nothing to play here; box 3 is the one you can listen to.
              """.trimIndent()
          )
  - create:
      box: condense_invents_nothing
      name: 2 - Invent nothing
      frame: { x: 60.0, y: 500.0, w: 400.0, h: 136.0 }
      properties:
        code: |

          import kotlin.random.Random

          // 2 - Invent nothing.
          //
          // The failure mode of a description-length search is naming a figure that is one step away
          // from the rhythm in front of it. This box runs that check live, on masks with no structure
          // put into them.
          //
          // POKE THIS: change `seed`, or `density` (one step in N is on), or `trials`. The count of
          // wrong namings has to stay 0 whatever you set, because every mask that came back as a
          // whole-span figure is recomputed here from bjorklund / kolakoskiMask and compared.
          val seed = 20260910
          val density = 3
          val trials = 300

          require(density > 0) { "density must be positive (one step in N is on)" }
          require(trials > 0) { "trials must be positive to compare compression" }

          val random = Random(seed)
          val euclidean = Regex("""^x\((\d+),(\d+)(?:,(\d+))?\)$""")
          val kolakoskian = Regex("""^x\(([kg])(\d+)(?:,(\d+))?\)$""")

          var named = 0
          var wrong = 0
          val ratios = mutableListOf<Double>()

          repeat(trials) {
              val mask = List(16) { random.nextInt(density) == 0 }
              val condensed = patCondense(mask)
              ratios += condensed.compression
              euclidean.matchEntire(condensed.notation)?.let { m ->
                  named++
                  val pulses = m.groupValues[1].toInt()
                  val steps = m.groupValues[2].toInt()
                  val offset = m.groupValues[3].ifEmpty { "0" }.toInt()
                  val figure = List(steps) { i -> bjorklund(pulses, steps)[((i + offset) % steps + steps) % steps] }
                  if (figure != mask) wrong++
              }
              kolakoskian.matchEntire(condensed.notation)?.let { m ->
                  named++
                  val reading = if (m.groupValues[1] == "k") KolakoskiReading.RUNS else KolakoskiReading.GAPS
                  val steps = m.groupValues[2].toInt()
                  val offset = m.groupValues[3].ifEmpty { "0" }.toInt()
                  if (kolakoskiMask(steps, reading, rotation = -offset) != mask) wrong++
              }
          }

          val median = ratios.sorted()[ratios.size / 2]
          val structured = patCondense(kolakoskiMask(16)).compression

          markdown(
              "main",
              """
              ### $trials random 16-step masks, seed $seed, one step in $density on

              | | |
              |---|---|
              | read as a single whole-span figure | **$named** |
              | of those, wrong | **$wrong** |
              | median compression | ${"%.3f".format(median)} |
              | the same number for `kolakoskiMask(16)` | ${"%.3f".format(structured)} |

              Noise compresses about ${"%.1f".format(median / structured)} times worse than the figure
              it is up against. That separation is the claim. If random masks compressed as well as
              structured ones, the search would be finding figures in nothing.

              None of this rests on the search's own opinion of itself: every string it returned was
              re-parsed and re-played before it was accepted, and the namings above are recomputed from
              the generators rather than read off the label.
              """.trimIndent()
          )
  - create:
      box: condense_from_audio
      name: 3 - Close the loop
      frame: { x: 60.0, y: 940.0, w: 400.0, h: 136.0 }
      properties:
        code: |


          // 3 - Close the loop: samples in, structure out.
          //
          // patTranscribe recovers a step grid from audio and writes it out one slot per step, which
          // is as far as listening alone can go. patCondense takes that string and returns the figure.
          // So this box renders a bar to samples, throws the pattern away, listens to the samples, and
          // gets the structure back.
          //
          // POKE THIS: change `bar`. Anything on an eight-step grid at MIDI 60 will be heard. Try
          // "60 ~ ~ 60 60 ~ 60 ~", which comes back as 60(k8), or "60 60 ~ 60 ~ ~ 60 ~", which has no
          // single figure and comes back as two halves.
          val bar = "60 ~ ~ 60 ~ 60 ~ ~"

          val audio = patRenderStereo(
              pat(bar),
              cycles = 1,
              cps = 0.5,
              voice = patVoice(decay = 0.06, sustain = 0.6)
          )
          val heard = patTranscribe(audio.mono, PatSoundDocs.SR, minHz = 180.0, maxHz = 420.0)
          val condensed = patCondense(heard)

          showPatRollOf(
              "played" to pat(bar),
              "heard" to pat(heard.miniNotation),
              "condensed: ${condensed.notation}" to pat(condensed.notation),
              title = "audio in, structure out",
              subtitle = "${audio.mono.size} samples at ${PatSoundDocs.SR} Hz, and no knowledge of what made them",
              cycles = 1,
              channel = "roll"
          )

          // The two players are the claim you can hear. The first is the EXACT buffer patTranscribe was
          // given - not a re-render of `bar`, the same floats - so what you hear is what was listened to.
          // The second is `condensed.notation` sent back through the same voice. If patCondense is right
          // they are the same bar, and the point of a shortest notation is that shortening it cost nothing.
          display(
              "played",
              afield.audio.engine.WavFloatArrayWriter.encodeStereo16(
                  audio.left, audio.right, PatSoundDocs.SR, normalizePeak = 0.9f
              )
          )
          showPatAudio(
              pat(condensed.notation),
              cycles = 1,
              cps = 0.5,
              voice = patVoice(decay = 0.06, sustain = 0.6),
              channel = "condensed"
          )

          markdown(
              "main",
              """
              ### From samples to a figure

              | | |
              |---|---|
              | played | `$bar` |
              | rendered | ${audio.mono.size} samples, ${"%.2f".format(audio.mono.size.toDouble() / PatSoundDocs.SR)} s |
              | grid recovered | ${heard.grid?.describe() ?: "none"} |
              | heard | `${heard.miniNotation}` |
              | **condensed** | **`${condensed.notation}`** |

              The `heard` row is everything `patTranscribe` can say on its own. The last row is the same
              bar after `patCondense(heard)`, and it names the figure. Nothing between the render and the
              transcription looked at `bar`.

              Four outputs, each on its own channel: **roll** draws the three lanes, **played** is the
              buffer that was actually transcribed, **condensed** is `${condensed.notation}` rendered
              back through the same voice, and this table is **main**. The two players should be the
              same bar — that is the claim, and it is the one part of this box you can check by ear.
              """.trimIndent()
          )
  - launch: { box: condense_basics, property: code }
  - launch: { box: condense_invents_nothing, property: code }
  - launch: { box: condense_from_audio, property: code }
  - await: { wait: pending }

before:
name: inertial_movement
description: >
  Inertial easing and the movement model built on it, as three boxes that get longer in that order.

  `inertial_hello` is the whole idea in five declarations: a `goal` that jumps, an `inertialTo` that
  chases it, and `showSignals` on the last 240 readings. `inertial_vs_restarting` adds one line —
  `startAt` on every frame — to show what restarting a blend costs, which is the thing inertial
  easing exists to avoid. `movement_playground` is the interactive one: drag an XY pad and a
  character chases it through `MovementState`, against a ghost of the same model with the smoothing
  turned off.

  Pure CPU, no Window and no Renderer, and all three are `loop { }`s, so they must be launched
  rather than executed. Nothing here needs an `AtomicReference`: the Framer runs one coroutine at a
  time, so a control callback and the loop never touch a `var` at once.

  Replayed end to end in CI: launch all three, run 90 real runloop frames, stop.
  After Daniel Holden's "Inertial Easing" and "A New Movement Model" (theorangeduck.com).
operations:
  - create:
      box: inertial_hello
      name: inertialTo — the whole idea
      frame: { x: 80.0, y: 80.0, w: 300.0, h: 100.0 }
      properties:
        code: |
          // Launch it (Alt-R). `goal` jumps between two values; reading `eased` arrives there with
          // inertia instead of snapping, and re-aims mid-flight without losing the speed it had.
          var goal = 0.0
          val eased = inertialTo<Double>(time = 0.4) { goal }
          val target = mutableListOf<Double>()
          val seen = mutableListOf<Double>()

          loop {
              goal = if ((frameNumber / 40) % 2 == 0) 1.0 else -1.0
              target += goal
              seen += eased()
              showSignals(
                  "goal" to target.takeLast(240),
                  "inertialTo" to seen.takeLast(240),
                  title = "one value, chasing a square wave",
                  xTitle = "frame",
              )
          }

  - create:
      box: inertial_vs_restarting
      name: what restarting a blend costs
      frame: { x: 80.0, y: 900.0, w: 310.0, h: 100.0 }
      properties:
        code: |
          /*
          The same target and two identical interpolants. The only difference is the `startAt`
          line: restarting a blend throws away the speed it had, which is what any one-shot ease
          does every time you re-aim it. Left alone, `inertialTo` re-solves and keeps that speed.
          */
          var goal = 0.0
          val carried = inertialTo<Double>(time = 0.4) { goal }
          val restarted = inertialTo<Double>(time = 0.4) { goal }

          val target = mutableListOf<Double>()
          val reSolved = mutableListOf<Double>()
          val fromRest = mutableListOf<Double>()

          loop {
              // A target that never holds still, so every single frame is a re-aim.
              goal = sin(frameNumber / 40.0) * 0.8
              (restarted as NeedsStartingValue<Double>).startAt(fromRest.lastOrNull() ?: 0.0)

              target += goal
              reSolved += carried()
              fromRest += restarted()

              showSignals(
                  "goal" to target.takeLast(300),
                  "left to re-solve" to reSolved.takeLast(300),
                  "restarted every frame" to fromRest.takeLast(300),
                  title = "re-solving a blend against restarting it",
                  xTitle = "frame",
              )
          }

  - create:
      box: movement_playground
      name: movement model — a character chasing a pad
      frame: { x: 80.0, y: 1750.0, w: 390.0, h: 100.0 }
      properties:
        code: |
          // Drag the XY pad; the character chases it. Every slider is live — one coroutine on the
          // Framer, so a plain `var` per control is all this needs.
          val view = Rect(0.0, 0.0, 720.0, 420.0)
          val home = Vector2f(360f, 210f)

          var pad = Vector2f(0.55f, -0.35f)
          var topSpeed = 420.0
          var acceleration = 1400.0
          var deceleration = 2200.0
          var turn = 2.0
          var directional = 0.0
          var halflife = 0.10
          var compensation = 1.0

          controlGroup("movement") {
              vec2Control(
                  "target",
                  initialValue = pad,
                  xRange = -1.0 to 1.0,
                  yRange = -1.0 to 1.0,
                  step = 0.02,
              ) {
                  pad = it
              }
              slider("top speed (px/s)", min = 80.0, max = 700.0, step = 10.0, initialValue = topSpeed) { topSpeed = it }
              slider("acceleration (px/s²)", min = 200.0, max = 4000.0, step = 50.0, initialValue = acceleration) {
                  acceleration = it
              }
              slider("deceleration (px/s²)", min = 200.0, max = 6000.0, step = 50.0, initialValue = deceleration) {
                  deceleration = it
              }
              slider("turn strength", min = 0.0, max = 16.0, step = 0.25, initialValue = turn) { turn = it }
              slider("directional acceleration", min = 0.0, max = 1.0, step = 0.05, initialValue = directional) {
                  directional = it
              }
              slider("smoothing half-life (s)", min = 0.0, max = 0.4, step = 0.005, initialValue = halflife) { halflife = it }
              slider("lag compensation", min = 0.0, max = 2.0, step = 0.05, initialValue = compensation) { compensation = it }
          }
          displayOpen("movement")

          val character = MovementState().apply { placeAt(home) }
          // The control arm: the same model with the smoothing taken off, so the distance between
          // the two characters is exactly what the smoothing is adding.
          val ghost = MovementState().apply { placeAt(home) }
          val trail = ArrayDeque<Vector2f>()

          // The stick: full deflection toward the target, easing off over the last few pixels.
          // Turning a position into a desired *velocity* is the whole interface to the model.
          fun stick(from: Vector2fc, to: Vector2fc): Vector2f {
              val gap = to - from
              val distance = gap.length()
              return if (distance < 3f) Vector2f() else gap * (min(topSpeed.toFloat(), distance * 5f) / distance)
          }

          // A character is where it is and where it is pointing.
          fun whisker(state: MovementState, length: Float) = fline {
              moveTo(state.position.x, state.position.y)
              lineTo(state.position.x + state.heading.x * length, state.position.y + state.heading.y * length)
          }

          loop {
              val target = Vector2f(home.x + pad.x * 300f, home.y - pad.y * 170f)
              val params = MovementParams(
                  acceleration = acceleration.toFloat(),
                  deceleration = deceleration.toFloat(),
                  turnStrength = turn.toFloat(),
                  directionalAcceleration = directional.toFloat(),
                  accelerationHalflife = halflife.toFloat(),
                  decelerationHalflife = (halflife * 0.5).toFloat(),
                  accelerationCompensation = compensation.toFloat(),
              )

              character.update(stick(character.position, target), 1f / 60f, params)
              ghost.update(
                  stick(ghost.position, target),
                  1f / 60f,
                  params.copy(accelerationHalflife = 0f, decelerationHalflife = 0f, accelerationCompensation = 0f),
              )

              trail.addLast(Vector2f(character.position))
              while (trail.size > 240) trail.removeFirst()

              val path = FLine()
              path.breakNext()
              trail.forEach { p -> path.lineTo(p.x, p.y) }

              showPlane(
                  [
                      fline {
                          moveTo(target.x - 10f, target.y)
                          lineTo(target.x + 10f, target.y)
                          moveTo(target.x, target.y - 10f)
                          lineTo(target.x, target.y + 10f)
                      }
                          .apply {
                              thickness = 1.5
                              color = vec(0.95, 0.85, 0.35, 1.0)
                          },
                      path.apply {
                          thickness = 1.5
                          color = vec(0.35, 0.75, 0.95, 1.0)
                      },
                      whisker(ghost, 14f).apply {
                          thickness = 1.0
                          color = vec(0.55, 0.55, 0.65, 1.0)
                      },
                      whisker(character, 22f).apply {
                          thickness = 2.5
                          color = vec(0.35, 0.95, 0.6, 1.0)
                      },
                  ],
                  window = view,
                  // Inside the 520px output panel, so this box's panel does not overlap its
                  // neighbour's in the column to the right.
                  width = 500,
                  height = 300,
                  title = "drag the pad — green is the model, grey is the same model unsmoothed",
                  caption = "%.0f px/s · %.0f px behind the target".format(character.speed, target.distance(character.position)),
              )
          }
  - launch: { box: inertial_hello, property: code }
  - launch: { box: inertial_vs_restarting, property: code }
  - launch: { box: movement_playground, property: code }
  - await: { wait: pending }
  - wait: { frames: 90 }
  - stop: { box: inertial_hello }
  - stop: { box: inertial_vs_restarting }
  - stop: { box: movement_playground }

after:
name: inertial_movement
description: >
  Inertial easing and the movement model built on it, as three boxes that get longer in that order.

  `inertial_hello` is the whole idea in five declarations: a `goal` that jumps, an `inertialTo` that
  chases it, and `showSignals` on the last 240 readings. `inertial_vs_restarting` adds one line —
  `startAt` on every frame — to show what restarting a blend costs, which is the thing inertial
  easing exists to avoid. `movement_playground` is the interactive one: drag an XY pad and a
  character chases it through `MovementState`, against a ghost of the same model with the smoothing
  turned off.

  Pure CPU, no Window and no Renderer, and all three are `loop { }`s, so they must be launched
  rather than executed. Nothing here needs an `AtomicReference`: the Framer runs one coroutine at a
  time, so a control callback and the loop never touch a `var` at once.

  Replayed end to end in CI: launch all three, run 90 real runloop frames, stop.
  After Daniel Holden's "Inertial Easing" and "A New Movement Model" (theorangeduck.com).
operations:
  - create:
      box: inertial_hello
      name: inertialTo — the whole idea
      frame: { x: 80.0, y: 80.0, w: 300.0, h: 100.0 }
      properties:
        code: |
          // Launch it (Alt-R). `goal` jumps between two values; reading `eased` arrives there with
          // inertia instead of snapping, and re-aims mid-flight without losing the speed it had.
          var goal = 0.0
          val eased = inertialTo<Double>(time = 0.4) { goal }
          val target = mutableListOf<Double>()
          val seen = mutableListOf<Double>()

          loop {
              goal = if ((frameNumber / 40) % 2 == 0) 1.0 else -1.0
              target += goal
              seen += eased()
              if (target.size > 240) {
                  target.removeAt(0)
                  seen.removeAt(0)
              }
              showSignals(
                  "goal" to target,
                  "inertialTo" to seen,
                  title = "one value, chasing a square wave",
                  xTitle = "frame",
              )
          }

  - create:
      box: inertial_vs_restarting
      name: what restarting a blend costs
      frame: { x: 80.0, y: 900.0, w: 310.0, h: 100.0 }
      properties:
        code: |
          /*
          The same target and two identical interpolants. The only difference is the `startAt`
          line: restarting a blend throws away the speed it had, which is what any one-shot ease
          does every time you re-aim it. Left alone, `inertialTo` re-solves and keeps that speed.
          */
          var goal = 0.0
          val carried = inertialTo<Double>(time = 0.4) { goal }
          val restarted = inertialTo<Double>(time = 0.4) { goal }

          val target = mutableListOf<Double>()
          val reSolved = mutableListOf<Double>()
          val fromRest = mutableListOf<Double>()

          loop {
              // A target that never holds still, so every single frame is a re-aim.
              goal = sin(frameNumber / 40.0) * 0.8
              (restarted as NeedsStartingValue<Double>).startAt(fromRest.lastOrNull() ?: 0.0)

              target += goal
              reSolved += carried()
              fromRest += restarted()
              if (target.size > 300) {
                  target.removeAt(0)
                  reSolved.removeAt(0)
                  fromRest.removeAt(0)
              }

              showSignals(
                  "goal" to target,
                  "left to re-solve" to reSolved,
                  "restarted every frame" to fromRest,
                  title = "re-solving a blend against restarting it",
                  xTitle = "frame",
              )
          }

  - create:
      box: movement_playground
      name: movement model — a character chasing a pad
      frame: { x: 80.0, y: 1750.0, w: 390.0, h: 100.0 }
      properties:
        code: |
          // Drag the XY pad; the character chases it. Every slider is live — one coroutine on the
          // Framer, so a plain `var` per control is all this needs.
          val view = Rect(0.0, 0.0, 720.0, 420.0)
          val home = Vector2f(360f, 210f)

          var pad = Vector2f(0.55f, -0.35f)
          var topSpeed = 420.0
          var acceleration = 1400.0
          var deceleration = 2200.0
          var turn = 2.0
          var directional = 0.0
          var halflife = 0.10
          var compensation = 1.0

          controlGroup("movement") {
              vec2Control(
                  "target",
                  initialValue = pad,
                  xRange = -1.0 to 1.0,
                  yRange = -1.0 to 1.0,
                  step = 0.02,
              ) {
                  pad = it
              }
              slider("top speed (px/s)", min = 80.0, max = 700.0, step = 10.0, initialValue = topSpeed) { topSpeed = it }
              slider("acceleration (px/s²)", min = 200.0, max = 4000.0, step = 50.0, initialValue = acceleration) {
                  acceleration = it
              }
              slider("deceleration (px/s²)", min = 200.0, max = 6000.0, step = 50.0, initialValue = deceleration) {
                  deceleration = it
              }
              slider("turn strength", min = 0.0, max = 16.0, step = 0.25, initialValue = turn) { turn = it }
              slider("directional acceleration", min = 0.0, max = 1.0, step = 0.05, initialValue = directional) {
                  directional = it
              }
              slider("smoothing half-life (s)", min = 0.0, max = 0.4, step = 0.005, initialValue = halflife) { halflife = it }
              slider("lag compensation", min = 0.0, max = 2.0, step = 0.05, initialValue = compensation) { compensation = it }
          }
          displayOpen("movement")

          val character = MovementState().apply { placeAt(home) }
          // The control arm: the same model with the smoothing taken off, so the distance between
          // the two characters is exactly what the smoothing is adding.
          val ghost = MovementState().apply { placeAt(home) }
          val trail = ArrayDeque<Vector2f>()

          // The stick: full deflection toward the target, easing off over the last few pixels.
          // Turning a position into a desired *velocity* is the whole interface to the model.
          fun stick(from: Vector2fc, to: Vector2fc): Vector2f {
              val gap = to - from
              val distance = gap.length()
              return if (distance < 3f) Vector2f() else gap * (min(topSpeed.toFloat(), distance * 5f) / distance)
          }

          // A character is where it is and where it is pointing.
          fun whisker(state: MovementState, length: Float) = fline {
              moveTo(state.position.x, state.position.y)
              lineTo(state.position.x + state.heading.x * length, state.position.y + state.heading.y * length)
          }

          loop {
              val target = Vector2f(home.x + pad.x * 300f, home.y - pad.y * 170f)
              val params = MovementParams(
                  acceleration = acceleration.toFloat(),
                  deceleration = deceleration.toFloat(),
                  turnStrength = turn.toFloat(),
                  directionalAcceleration = directional.toFloat(),
                  accelerationHalflife = halflife.toFloat(),
                  decelerationHalflife = (halflife * 0.5).toFloat(),
                  accelerationCompensation = compensation.toFloat(),
              )

              character.update(stick(character.position, target), 1f / 60f, params)
              ghost.update(
                  stick(ghost.position, target),
                  1f / 60f,
                  params.copy(accelerationHalflife = 0f, decelerationHalflife = 0f, accelerationCompensation = 0f),
              )

              trail.addLast(Vector2f(character.position))
              while (trail.size > 240) trail.removeFirst()

              val path = FLine()
              path.breakNext()
              trail.forEach { p -> path.lineTo(p.x, p.y) }

              showPlane(
                  [
                      fline {
                          moveTo(target.x - 10f, target.y)
                          lineTo(target.x + 10f, target.y)
                          moveTo(target.x, target.y - 10f)
                          lineTo(target.x, target.y + 10f)
                      }
                          .apply {
                              thickness = 1.5
                              color = vec(0.95, 0.85, 0.35, 1.0)
                          },
                      path.apply {
                          thickness = 1.5
                          color = vec(0.35, 0.75, 0.95, 1.0)
                      },
                      whisker(ghost, 14f).apply {
                          thickness = 1.0
                          color = vec(0.55, 0.55, 0.65, 1.0)
                      },
                      whisker(character, 22f).apply {
                          thickness = 2.5
                          color = vec(0.35, 0.95, 0.6, 1.0)
                      },
                  ],
                  window = view,
                  // Inside the 520px output panel, so this box's panel does not overlap its
                  // neighbour's in the column to the right.
                  width = 500,
                  height = 300,
                  title = "drag the pad — green is the model, grey is the same model unsmoothed",
                  caption = "%.0f px/s · %.0f px behind the target".format(character.speed, target.distance(character.position)),
              )
          }
  - launch: { box: inertial_hello, property: code }
  - launch: { box: inertial_vs_restarting, property: code }
  - launch: { box: movement_playground, property: code }
  - await: { wait: pending }
  - wait: { frames: 90 }
  - stop: { box: inertial_hello }
  - stop: { box: inertial_vs_restarting }
  - stop: { box: movement_playground }
